3. A given group may act in many different ways on the same space. Here are three different actions of $\mathbb{Z}_{2}$ on the torus. Take the torus T in $\mathbb{E}^{3}$ formed by rotating the circle $( x-3 )^{2}+z^{2}=1$ about the z axis. Let g denote the generator of $\mathbb{Z}_{2}$ and define:

(a) $g(x,y,z) = (x, - y, - z),$ rotation of $T$ through $\pi$ about the x axis;

(b) $g(x,y,z)=(-x,-y,z),$ rotation of T through $\pi$ about the z axis;

(c) $g(x,y,z)=(-x,-y,-z),$ reflexion of $T$ in the origin.

Each of these homeomorphisms determines an action of $\mathbb{Z}_{2}$ on T. The orbit spaces are the sphere, torus, and Klein bottle respectively, and Fig. 4.3 shows why. In each case g interchanges the cylinders $C_{1}$ and $C_{2}$ . So in order to form the orbit space we can ignore $C_{2}$ and simply make the appropriate identifications on the boundary circles of $C_{1}.$

4. If $G$ is a topological group and $H$ a subgroup of $G$ , then $H$ acts on $G$ by left translation. The homeomorphism induced by an element $h$ of $H$ is $L_{h}$ , i.e., $h(g)=L_{h}(g)=hg$ , and the associated function $H\times G\to G$ is continuous, since multiplication in $G$ is continuous. Two elements $g,g^{\prime}$ lie in the same orbit if and only if $g^{\prime}\in Hg$ . Therefore the orbits are the right cosets of $H$ in $G$ .

We also have a 'right action' of H on G given by the map

$$
\begin{array}{l} H \times G \rightarrow G \\ (h, g) \mapsto R _ {f ^ {- 1}} (g) \\ \end{array}
$$
